  :adr/last_verified "2026-07-25"
  :adr/body "# ADR-2607251500: AliExpress product/seller/price observation dataset (EDN + DataScript), and the measured limits of automating its collection\n\n**Status**: accepted\n**Date**: 2026-07-25\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization for docs/dataset work)\n**Related**: `90-docs/adr/2605270000-hakken-oem-product-discovery-bmc-lean.edn` (OEM/dropship discovery design that names AliExpress/1688/Alibaba as sources but never implemented a fetcher — this ADR's finding explains why a naive fetcher would not have worked anyway)\n\n## Context\n\nOwner request: collect AliExpress product, seller (company), and price data into a form queryable via EDN/DataScript, scoped to products that look plausible for \"sedori\" (Japanese retail-arbitrage resale) — no fixed keyword, and no fixed item count; instead \"build a continuous collection mechanism\".\n\nBefore designing anything, existing infra was checked (repo convention: don't build a parallel system if one exists). Three related repos were found —\n`orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-hakken`, `orgs/gftdcojp/ai-gftd-apps-gftdcojp/60-apps/ai-gftd-project-hakken` (design-only, Python pod pruned 2026-07-07, explicitly \"cljc port はしない\"), and `orgs/etzhayyim/root/60-apps/etzhayyim-project-hakken` — all three converge on the same `supplier_search` node returning **2 hardcoded stub candidates**; none has a working AliExpress fetch path. `etzhayyim-project-hakken`'s `nodes/supplier_search.cljc` has an injectable `*aliexpress-search*` var gated on an `ALIEXPRESS_API_URL` env var pointing at a **non-existent placeholder XRPC endpoint** — never a real integration. This confirmed a new, standalone dataset was the right call rather than wiring into hakken.\n\n### Feasibility measurement (this is the substantive finding)\n\nBefore committing to a design, the two plausible collection mechanisms were tested directly:\n\n1. **Plain HTTP fetch** (`curl` with a normal desktop User-Agent) against `https://www.aliexpress.com/w/wholesale-gadgets.html` — the response is a 2.4KB JS redirect to `.../_____tmd_____/punish?x5secdata=...`, AliExpress's bot-challenge wall. This is unambiguous: non-browser clients are actively fingerprinted and blocked. Writing code to get past this (header/TLS-fingerprint spoofing, solving the challenge, proxy rotation) would be bot-detection evasion — a hard line, not a design tradeoff, regardless of how convenient a cron-friendly scraper would be.\n2. **`WebFetch` tool** (renders JS, unlike raw `curl`) against the same URL — succeeded on the first two calls in a short window, returning real product cards (title, price, original price, discount %, rating, sold count). A third call asking for per-product seller/store identifiers on the *same* listing page came back negative — store name is not present on the listing/search page at all, only on the product detail page. Further `WebFetch` calls in the same session, both to the same URL and to five different plausible category-listing URLs, then returned **empty content** — most likely `WebFetch`'s own fetch path drawing the same anti-bot scrutiny after a handful of requests in quick succession (a retry with the *original* URL succeeded again, but that is consistent with `WebFetch`'s documented 15-minute result cache serving a stale hit rather than a fresh fetch). No attempt was made to retry past this with different tactics — repeatedly varying the request to find one that slips through is itself evasion-shaped behavior and was deliberately not pursued.\n3. **`WebFetch` against a product detail page** (`ja.aliexpress.com/item/<id>.html`) — returned only the page's static footer shell. Detail pages are client-rendered (SPA); a non-JS-executing-enough fetch never sees the product/seller data at all.\n4. **Claude-in-Chrome** (a real, human-paired browser session) against the same detail pages — rendered normally, no challenge encountered, seller/store name (plus feedback % and follower count) directly readable via `get_page_text` after scrolling. This worked cleanly for 3 products in a row with no sign of blocking.\n\n**Conclusion**: listing-level data (title/price/discount/rating/sold-count) is obtainable via `WebFetch` at low, human-paced frequency; seller/company data requires an actual rendered browser session (Claude-in-Chrome) and is inherently interactive-session-only. Neither mechanism is suitable for an unattended, high-frequency cron scraper — the site's own anti-bot posture forecloses that without evasion, which is off the table on its own terms.\n\n## Decision\n\nBuild `90-docs/aliexpress-sedori/` as a standalone EDN dataset, following this repo's established append-only-ledger-over-DataScript pattern (`90-docs/business/canvas-ledger.edn`, `90-docs/design-quality/design-quality-ledger.edn`, `90-docs/adr-ledger/adr-ledger.edn`) rather than a new west-registered repo — this is a growing observation log, not a piece of software with its own deploy/release lifecycle.\n\n- **Schema**: one EDN map per line in `observations-ledger.edn`. `:event/type :product-observed` (title/url/price/orig-price/discount-pct/rating/sold-count-min/label, tagged with `:event/category` = the search keyword used) or `:event/type :seller-observed` (seller name/feedback-pct/followers, joined back to a product via a shared `:product/id` value — the same join-on-shared-value approach `manifest/edn-query.cljs` uses to fold ADR-ledger amendments against base ADRs, since DataScript here has no real `:db.type/ref` bootstrapping and doesn't need one for this).\n- **No separate mutable \"catalog\" file.** \"Current state per product\" (`query.cljs latest`) and a sedori-candidate shortlist (`query.cljs candidates`) are computed from the ledger at query time, not materialized — matching how this repo already treats ADR amendments and design-quality scores as append-only history with a query-time projection, rather than a rewritten snapshot.\n- **`append.cljs`** mirrors `scripts/adr-ledger-append.cljs`'s discipline: exclusive-lock file, monotonic `:event/seq`, batch input (a vector of event maps) since a single collection pass yields many events at once, `--verify` mode (duplicate/missing/non-monotonic seq, orphan `:seller-observed` events with no matching product).\n- **`query.cljs candidates`** applies an objective, measured-signal-only filter (sold-count ≥ 1000, rating ≥ 4.5, discount ≥ 50%) and explicitly does **not** compute a profitability/margin score — AliExpress-side data alone cannot support that (no resale-side reference price was collected), and fabricating one would violate this repo's standing system-dynamics rule against presenting uncomputed values as measured (`ADR-2607203000`'s `:uncomputable-until-measured` convention, applied here even though this isn't a system-dynamics model).\n- **Collection is manual/opportunistic, not scheduled.** No `RemoteTrigger`/`CronCreate` routine was created for this. The header comments in `append.cljs`/`query.cljs` state plainly that a future run hitting the challenge page again should record zero results and say so, not try harder to get past it. (No standalone `README.md` was added under `90-docs/aliexpress-sedori/` — `90-docs` is EDN-only per ADR-2607171600; usage/schema documentation instead lives in this ADR body and in the two scripts' header comments.)\n\n## Verification\n\nSeeded the ledger with real, directly-observed data from one category (`gadgets`, `event/run-id \"2026-07-25-seed-gadgets\"`): 12 `:product-observed` events from a genuine `WebFetch` listing-page read, plus 3 `:seller-observed` enrichment events from live Claude-in-Chrome detail-page reads (Steel Riot Jewelry Store / James Lighter Store / elough Official Store, with feedback % and follower counts). Ran end-to-end: `append.cljs --batch` → `append.cljs --verify` (`events=15 products=12 problems=0`) → `query.cljs count` / `latest` / `candidates` (6 of 12 products clear the sold/rating/discount thresholds) → an arbitrary raw Datalog query joining seller name to feedback %/followers, all returning correct real values. No fabricated rows were added — every number traces to an actual `WebFetch` or Claude-in-Chrome read performed in this session.\n\n## Consequences\n\n(+) A real, working, queryable dataset exists now, seeded with genuine data, not a design doc promising future collection.\n\n(+) The bot-detection finding is now a citable fact for this workspace (mirrors how other ADRs record a measured technical constraint once so it isn't re-discovered from scratch) — anyone tempted to build an AliExpress cron scraper later should read this ADR first.\n\n(+) The append-only/query-time-projection shape means future collection passes are pure additions — no risk of clobbering prior observations, consistent with this repo's broader \"don't hand-rewrite ledgers\" discipline.\n\n(-) Growth is opportunistic and unscheduled — there is no guaranteed refresh cadence, and seller enrichment in particular only happens when an interactive session with a connected browser does it by hand. This is an honest limitation, not a bug to silently patch over with a fragile always-on scraper.\n\n(-) `sold/count-min` is a parsed lower bound from AliExpress's own rounded label (e.g. \"50,000+\"), not an exact count — acceptable for a coarse arbitrage-candidate filter, not for anything requiring precision.\n\n(-) No cross-reference to resale-side pricing (Amazon/Mercari/Yahoo Auctions Japan) exists in this dataset — `candidates` flags AliExpress-side popularity/discount signals only, not actual arbitrage margin. Adding that would need its own scope and its own ADR.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n| Option | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|\n| Wire into `etzhayyim-project-hakken`'s `supplier_search.cljc` stub | ❌ (for now) | Owner explicitly chose a standalone dataset over extending hakken; also that pipeline's AT-record/PDS storage is a heavier integration than this task needs |\n| Write an nbb/`curl`-based HTTP scraper for continuous automated collection | ❌ | Measured: immediately hits AliExpress's CAPTCHA/JS-challenge wall; making it \"work\" requires bot-detection evasion, a hard line |\n| Schedule a `RemoteTrigger`/cron routine calling `WebFetch` periodically | ❌ (not yet) | `WebFetch` itself showed signs of being rate-limited/challenged after a handful of requests in one session; scheduling it into an unattended recurring job would be pushing exactly the kind of repeated automated access the site's anti-bot system exists to stop, even though each individual call uses a sanctioned tool |\n| Register for the AliExpress Open Platform / Affiliate API | ✅ (recommended follow-up, owner action required) | The actual legitimate path to robust continuous access; requires the account owner to sign up, out of scope for an agent to do unilaterally |\n| Materialize a rewritten \"current catalog\" file instead of query-time projection | ❌ | Diverges from this repo's established append-only-ledger pattern for exactly this kind of growing, timestamped observational data |\n\n## References\n\n- `90-docs/aliexpress-sedori/append.cljs`, `query.cljs`, `observations-ledger.edn`\n- `90-docs/adr/2605270000-hakken-oem-product-discovery-bmc-lean.edn`\n- `orgs/etzhayyim/root/60-apps/etzhayyim-project-hakken/lg-clj/src/lg_hakken/nodes/supplier_search.cljc`\n- `scripts/adr-ledger-append.cljs` (pattern this dataset's `append.cljs` mirrors)\n- `90-docs/business/canvas-ledger.edn`, `90-docs/design-quality/design-quality-ledger.edn` (prior art for the append-only-ledger-over-DataScript shape)\n\n\n## 改訂履歴\n\n（2026-07-25 に `90-docs/adr-ledger/adr-ledger.edn` から統合。以後この文書は最新状態のみを表し、履歴は git が持つ。）

### 2026-07-25 — amend

転売側参照価格(:resale-price-observed)を追加、店舗名12/12完了、一覧価格と詳細価格の系統的乖離を実測

2026-07-25 二次収集パス。(1) 転売側の相場取得経路を4つ実測: Yahoo!ショッピング検索は WebFetch で通る(唯一の実働経路、4クエリすべて成功、実売価格の分布を取得)。楽天は HTTP 403、Amazon.co.jp は空応答、メルカリは検索結果がJS描画で WebFetch ではページ枠のみ取得(実ブラウザなら可)。(2) :event/type :resale-price-observed を append.cljs の valid-types に追加し、query.cljs に margin コマンドを追加。相場は同一商品の売値ではなくキーワード検索のカテゴリ分布(min/median/max + sample-size + total-results + :resale/same-product? false + note)として記録し、関税・輸入消費税・国内送料・販売手数料・返品率を一切含まないことを margin の docstring と出力の両方に明記した(利益率と誤読させないため)。(3) 店舗名が欠けていた9商品を Claude-in-Chrome で詳細ページ巡回し補完、12商品すべてに :seller-observed が付いた(James Lighter Store が3商品を扱っており seller join が実データで機能することを確認)。出品者ページが複数出品者の集約面になっている7商品は feedback%/フォロワー数を表示しないため、その2属性は欠落させ :seller/aggregate-page true を付けた(捏造しない)。(4) 重要な実測: 一覧ページ価格と詳細ページ価格が両方ある11商品のうち6商品で価格が食い違い、最大2.5倍の差があった(ガーリックプレス 一覧150円 vs 詳細379円、バッジリール 100円 vs 235円、ポケモンキーホルダー 110円 vs 273円、ダックキーホルダー 100円 vs 238円、魚うろこ取り 140円 vs 285円、反射ステッカー 130円 vs 140円)。一致した4商品はいずれも専用の出品者情報ブロック(feedback%/フォロワー数あり)を持つ通常の単独出品ページで、乖離した商品は :seller/aggregate-page true の集約面だった。したがって仕入れ値として安い一覧価格を採ると利ざやを過大評価するため、query.cljs の purchasable は :detail-browse を優先する保守的な実装にした。append-only なのでどちらの観測も破棄せず両方 ledger に残る。(5) 現在 events=37 products=12 sellers-known=10 margin算出可能=4件。verify OK。
"
